std.debug — Assertions and fatal-error helpers
===============================================

The `std.debug` module provides the assertion and
unreachable-path helpers that the rest of the standard library
and user code use to signal violated invariants and
unfinished work. It is the foundational module that every
other `std` module depends on.

`panic(msg: string)` is a Sysl language builtin, not defined
here: it halts execution with the given message. `std.debug`
layers three small helpers on top of it.

Contract summary:

- `assert(cond, msg)` — panic if `cond` is false, otherwise no-op.
- `unreachable(msg)` — panic with a `"unreachable: "` prefix.
- `todo(msg)` — panic with a `"todo: "` prefix.

These funnel through `panic`, so they all trigger the normal
test-failure path under `sysl test` and the normal halt path
under `sysl run`.


    module std.debug


assert
------

`assert(cond, msg)` is a no-op when `cond` is true and calls
`panic(msg)` when it is false. The message should describe the
invariant that was violated, not the condition that was
checked. Prefer `"buffer index out of range"` over
`"i >= 0 && i < len"`.

Assertions are not stripped in any build profile. Sysl has no
release-mode assertion removal: if you do not want the check
at runtime, do not write it. Silently-disappearing checks are
a common bug source and `std.debug.assert` does not introduce
them.

    assert(cond: bool, msg: string) -> unit
        if !cond then panic(msg)


unreachable
-----------

`unreachable(msg)` panics unconditionally with a `"unreachable: "`
prefix. It marks code paths the programmer asserts cannot
execute — typically the default arm of an exhaustive match, or
the fall-through after a non-returning call. The message
should explain *why* the programmer believes the path is
unreachable.

    unreachable(msg: string) -> unit
        panic("unreachable: " + msg)


todo
----

`todo(msg)` panics unconditionally with a `"todo: "` prefix.
It marks code paths that have not yet been implemented. Code
containing `todo` compiles and links but fails loudly at
runtime if exercised. The message should describe what needs
to be done.

`todo` is distinct from `unreachable` so that tooling can tell
"not implemented yet" from "cannot happen".

    todo(msg: string) -> unit
        panic("todo: " + msg)


Tests
=====

    #test
    test_assert_true_is_noop() -> unit
        assert(true, "should not fire")

    #test(should_panic: "math is broken")
    test_assert_false_panics() -> unit
        assert(false, "math is broken")

    #test(should_panic: "unreachable: dead branch")
    test_unreachable_panics_with_prefix() -> unit
        unreachable("dead branch")

    #test(should_panic: "todo: implement me")
    test_todo_panics_with_prefix() -> unit
        todo("implement me")


Design notes
------------

**No source location capture yet.** Rust's `panic!`, `assert!`,
`unreachable!`, and `todo!` macros capture `file!()`/`line!()`
automatically. Sysl has no macro system, so location capture
would require dedicated compiler support — an intrinsic that
inserts location information at the call site. This is
deferred; when added, it will be a non-breaking change.

**No formatted messages.** These functions take a plain
`string`, not a format string. Users who want a formatted
message should build it with `std.builder` or Sysl's string
interpolation and pass the result to `assert` / `unreachable`
/ `todo`. This keeps `std.debug` dependency-free.

**No recovery.** `panic` is terminal. Code that needs to fail
recoverably should return a `Result`, not panic.
